  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{\u2033}+x{y}^{\u2032}-4y=0.\n$$\n\nLet $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{\u2033}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ Thus, the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{2}}+{c}_{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is proportional to $6x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is the constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)+x(\\alpha )-4(\\alpha x) & =6x \\\\ (\\alpha -4\\alpha )x & =6x \\\\ -3\\alpha x & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get $\\alpha =-2.$\n\nHence, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{2}}+{c}_{2}{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
